__mdna__0018", "ticker": "JPM", "form": "10-Q", "fiscal_label": "Q1 FY2025", "period_end": "2025-03-31", "section": "mdna", "score": 0.3764, "filing_name": "JPMorgan Chase & Co 10-Q Q1 FY2025", "sec_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/19617/000001961725000421/jpm-20250331.htm", "truncated": false}, {"chunk_id": "JPM_10Q_2025-03-31__mdna__0007", "ticker": "JPM", "form": "10-Q", "fiscal_label": "Q1 FY2025", "period_end": "2025-03-31", "section": "mdna", "score": 0.3555, "filing_name": "JPMorgan Chase & Co 10-Q Q1 FY2025", "sec_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/19617/000001961725000421/jpm-20250331.htm", "truncated": false}], "collapsed_near_duplicates": 46, "dropped_candidates": 532, "token_estimate": 31915, "gaps": [], "degraded": []}, "answer": {"direct_answer": "Apple's FY2025 10-K (fiscal year ended September 27, 2025) groups its risks under macroeconomic and industry, business, legal and regulatory, and financial headings, including global economic conditions, trade restrictions, and a supply chain concentrated with outsourcing partners [AAPL_10K_2025-09-27__risk_factors__0000] [AAPL_10K_2025-09-27__risk_factors__0002]. Tesla's FY2025 10-K (fiscal year ended December 31, 2025) stresses production ramp and cost execution, including driver assistance, autonomy, and Cybercab, plus heavy dependence on Elon Musk [TSLA_10K_2025-12-31__risk_factors__0000] [TSLA_10K_2025-12-31__risk_factors__0019]. JPMorgan's FY2025 10-K (fiscal year ended December 31, 2025) leads with legal and regulatory risk and also lists political risk and market risk among its principal risk factors [JPM_10K_2025-12-31__risk_factors__0000]. All three cite competition and cybersecurity or data security, but Apple and Tesla's risks center on products, manufacturing and supply chains, while JPMorgan's center on regulation and markets [AAPL_10K_2025-09-27__risk_factors__0018] [TSLA_10K_2025-12-31__risk_factors__0019]. The filings do not rank their risks, so no ordering by severity is given, and the retrieved JPMorgan chunks cover only part of its summary list.", "company_findings": [{"ticker": "AAPL", "company": "Apple Inc", "findings": [{"point": "Adverse global and regional economic conditions, with a majority of sales outside the U.S. and most supplier facilities located outside the U.S., can reduce demand for Apple's products and services.", "citations": ["AAPL_10K_2025-09-27__risk_factors__0000"], "unverified": false}, {"point": "A significant majority of manufacturing is done by outsourcing partners in Asia, and restrictions on international trade such as tariffs can materially affect its business and supply chain.", "citations": ["AAPL_10K_2025-09-27__risk_factors__0002"], "unverified": false}, {"point": "Apple faces intense competition, must manage frequent product transitions, and is exposed to cybersecurity attacks and complex, changing laws and regulations.", "citations": ["AAPL_10K_2025-09-27__risk_factors__0007", "AAPL_10K_2025-09-27__risk_factors__0018"], "unverified": false}]}, {"ticker": "TSLA", "company": "Tesla Inc", "findings": [{"point": "Tesla may face issues or delays in developing, launching and ramping production and may be unable to control manufacturing costs, with its future business depending on driver assistance, autonomy and Cybercab.", "citations": ["TSLA_10K_2025-12-31__risk_factors__0000"], "unverified": false}, {"point": "Tesla faces strong competition from a growing list of established and new competitors in electric vehicles, self-driving technology and energy products.", "citations": ["TSLA_10K_2025-12-31__risk_factors__0009"], "unverified": false}, {"point": "Tesla is highly dependent on Elon Musk, who does not devote his full time to Tesla, and its systems are potentially vulnerable to cyber-attacks.", "citations": ["TSLA_10K_2025-12-31__risk_factors__0019"], "unverified": false}]}, {"ticker": "JPM", "company": "JPMorgan Chase & Co", "findings": [{"point": "The principal risk factors begin with legal 